==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|ydropsy}}, from {{der|en|fro|idropsie}}, {{m|fro|ydropisie}}, from {{der|en|la|hydropisis}}, from {{der|en|grc|ὕδρωψ|}}, from {{m|grc|ὕδωρ|t=water}}. {{doublet|en|hydropsy|hydrops}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|dropsies}} 

# {{lb|en|pathology}} Swelling, [[edema]], often from chronic obstructive pulmonary disease ([[COPD]]).
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|IV|i|passage={{smallcaps|Caliban}}: The '''dropſie''' drowne this foole,{{...}}}}
#* '''1820''', {{w|Percy Bysshe Shelley}}, ''Oedipus Tyrannus; Or, Swellfoot The Tyrant: A Tragedy in Two Acts'':
#*: Your sacred Majesty, he has the '''dropsy''';—<br>We shall find pints of hydatids in’s liver,<br>He has not half an inch of wholesome fat<br>Upon his carious ribs—
#* '''1911''', Joseph Addison, [[S:1911 Encyclopædia Britannica/Addison, Joseph|Encyclopædia Britannica]]:
#*: The disease under which Addison laboured appears to have been asthma. It became more violent after his retirement from office, and was now accompanied by '''dropsy'''.
#* '''2020''', Pat Goodmann, ''[http://blog.wolfpark.org/happy-90th-birthday-dr-klinghammer/ Wolf Park Blog: Happy 90th Birthday Dr. Klinghammer]'':
#*: Like someone moving back in space until the earth appears as a tiny mote of light, he managed to distance himself from the immediate discomfort of pipes with '''dropsy''', defiant plumbing tools, and the overly familiar fiberglass, by musing aloud on the evolutionary history of humans that allowed the two of us to communicate verbally, creating a word picture of the problem and how to fix it.

